<div class="mk-crumbbar"><div class="mk-wrap in"><a href="#">Markets</a><span class="sep">&rsaquo;</span><a href="/markets/optical-sensors">Optical &amp; Sensors</a><span class="sep">&rsaquo;</span><span class="here">Microdisplay</span></div></div><div class="mk-spoke-grid"><aside class="mk-side"><div class="side-head"><span class="side-head-label">Optical &amp; Sensors products</span><button type="button" class="mk-side-toggle" aria-label="Collapse sidebar"><span class="chev"><i data-lucide="chevron-right" style="width:13px;height:13px;"></i></span></button></div><a class="s" href="/markets/optical-sensors/camera-modules"><span class="abbr">CM</span><span class="label">Camera Modules</span></a><a class="s" href="/markets/optical-sensors/mems-sensors"><span class="abbr">MS</span><span class="label">MEMS Sensors</span></a><a class="s active" href="/markets/optical-sensors/microdisplay"><span class="abbr">MD</span><span class="label">Microdisplay</span></a><div class="helper"><div class="h">Not sure which fits?</div><p>Tell us what the module has to see or display and we&rsquo;ll point you to the right line.</p><a class="cta" href="/contact">Work with us <i data-lucide="arrow-right" style="width:12px;height:12px;"></i></a></div></aside><div class="mk-spoke-main"><section class="mk-hero" data-hana-bg="flow" data-variant="light" data-fade="none"><div class="mk-wrap mk-hero-in"><div><div class="mk-eyebrow">Optical &amp; Sensors &middot; Microdisplay</div><h1 class="mk-h1">Microdisplay manufacturing — LCOS and HTPS</h1><p class="mk-lede">A microdisplay is a liquid-crystal panel measured in millimeters rather than inches: LCOS builds the panel onto a silicon backplane and works by reflecting light off it, while HTPS uses high-temperature polysilicon and passes light through. In both, the panel sits directly in an optical path, so a particle trapped during cell assembly or a few microns of misalignment is a defect the optics will magnify rather than hide.</p><div class="mk-btns"><a href="/contact" class="hana-btn hana-btn-primary" style="padding:12px 22px;font-size:12px;">Talk to us <i data-lucide="arrow-right" style="width:14px;height:14px;margin-left:6px;"></i></a><a href="#specialize-microdisplay" class="hana-btn hana-btn-secondary" style="padding:12px 22px;font-size:12px;">What we specialize in</a></div></div><div class="mk-hero-media"><div class="mk-ph" style="aspect-ratio:auto;height:420px;border-bottom:0;"><div style="position:absolute;top:10px;left:12px;width:18px;height:18px;opacity:.4;z-index:1;"><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="var(--hana-blue)" stroke-width="1.5"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"></circle><path d="M12 3v4M12 17v4M3 12h4M17 12h4"></path></svg></div><span class="cap">[ Microdisplay &mdash; photography needed ]</span></div></div></div></section><section id="specialize-microdisplay" data-hana-bg="hex" data-variant="light" data-fade="none" style="background:var(--bg);border-top:1px solid var(--line);position:relative;overflow:hidden;"><div class="mk-wrap" style="position:relative;z-index:1;padding-top:64px;padding-bottom:60px;"><div style="max-width:700px;margin-bottom:26px;"><h2 class="mk-h2">What we specialize in</h2><p class="mk-body">Hana builds LCOS and HTPS microdisplays from wafer through to packaged panel, on cleanroom lines under controlled ESD handling. It is uncommon work, and we have run it for years.</p></div><div class="mk-sheet"><div class="row"><div class="lab"><span class="n">01</span><span class="t">LCOS microdisplays</span></div><div class="desc">liquid-crystal-on-silicon panels built onto the silicon backplane and assembled through to a packaged, handled display.</div></div><div class="row"><div class="lab"><span class="n">02</span><span class="t">HTPS microdisplays</span></div><div class="desc">high-temperature polysilicon transmissive panels, assembled and packaged to the same optical-grade discipline.</div></div><div class="row"><div class="lab"><span class="n">03</span><span class="t">LCD processing and packaging</span></div><div class="desc">the cell processing and display packaging steps behind both panel types, available as a scope in their own right.</div></div><div class="row"><div class="lab"><span class="n">04</span><span class="t">Wafer processing and dicing</span></div><div class="desc">backplane wafer handling and singulation ahead of panel assembly, so the optical build starts from a controlled part.</div></div></div><div class="mk-caplinks"><p class="note">Process depth sits on the capability page: Micro Assembly owns the die-level handling and bonding accuracies a panel build depends on, across the wider microelectronic assembly range.</p><div class="links"><a href="/capabilities/microelectronic-assembly/micro-assembly">Explore Micro Assembly <i data-lucide="arrow-right" style="width:12px;height:12px;"></i></a></div></div></div></section><section class="mk-dark"><div class="mk-wrap in"><div style="max-width:820px;"><h2 class="mk-h2 on-dark">Cleanliness sits in the optical path</h2><p class="mk-body on-dark" style="margin:0 0 14px;">On most assemblies a particle is a contamination risk. On a microdisplay it is in the light path, and whatever the panel is doing, the optics downstream make it bigger. That is why the discipline on this product is environmental before it is mechanical: cleanroom lines under controlled ESD handling, controlled part handling from wafer through cell assembly, and packaging steps designed to close the panel without introducing anything onto its surface. The alignment tolerances are tight enough that the process has to be repeatable rather than corrected afterwards — a microdisplay cannot be reworked back to clean.</p></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--hana-blue-tint);border-top:1px solid var(--line);border-bottom:1px solid var(--line);"><div class="mk-wrap" style="padding-top:56px;padding-bottom:56px;"><div style="max-width:700px;margin-bottom:24px;"><div class="mk-eyebrow">Footprint</div><h2 class="mk-h2 sm">Where we build</h2></div><div class="mk-locs"><a class="mk-loc" href="/locations/ohio"><span class="co"><i data-lucide="map-pin" style="width:13px;height:13px;color:var(--hana-blue);"></i><span class="hana-spec-label">USA</span></span><div class="nm">Ohio</div><div class="note">LCOS and HTPS microdisplays</div><span class="cta">View plant <i data-lucide="arrow-right" style="width:12px;height:12px;"></i></span></a><a class="mk-loc" href="/locations/ayutthaya"><span class="co"><i data-lucide="map-pin" style="width:13px;height:13px;color:var(--hana-blue);"></i><span class="hana-spec-label">Thailand</span></span><div class="nm">Ayutthaya</div><div class="note">micro-assembly, wafer-level processing</div><span class="cta">View plant <i data-lucide="arrow-right" style="width:12px;height:12px;"></i></span></a></div><p style="margin:18px 0 0;"><a href="/locations" class="hana-link" style="font-size:11px;letter-spacing:0.1em;">All locations <i data-lucide="arrow-right" style="width:13px;height:13px;"></i></a></p></div></section><section style="background:#fff;border-top:1px solid var(--line);"><div class="mk-wrap" style="padding-top:56px;padding-bottom:60px;"><div style="max-width:700px;margin-bottom:22px;"><div class="mk-eyebrow">FAQ</div><h2 class="mk-h2 sm">Frequently asked questions</h2></div><div style="max-width:860px;"><details class="mk-faq" open><summary><span class="mk-faq-mk"><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12h14"></path><path d="m12 5 7 7-7 7"></path></svg></span><span style="flex:1;">What is an LCOS or HTPS microdisplay?</span></summary><p class="mk-faq-a">Both are miniature liquid-crystal display panels. LCOS — liquid crystal on silicon — is built onto a silicon backplane and reflects light; HTPS uses a high-temperature polysilicon panel and transmits light through it. Which one a program uses is a design decision; Hana builds both.</p></details><details class="mk-faq"><summary><span class="mk-faq-mk"><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12h14"></path><path d="m12 5 7 7-7 7"></path></svg></span><span style="flex:1;">What applications use Hana-built microdisplays?</span></summary><p class="mk-faq-a">Projectors and rear-projection displays, flight simulators, and wavelength-selective switches in optical networking equipment. We build the display; what the finished system does with it belongs to our customers.</p></details><details class="mk-faq"><summary><span class="mk-faq-mk"><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12h14"></path><path d="m12 5 7 7-7 7"></path></svg></span><span style="flex:1;">Why is cleanliness the deciding factor in display yield?</span></summary><p class="mk-faq-a">Because the panel sits in the optical path. A particle or a handling mark on a microdisplay is not hidden by the optics around it — it is magnified by them, and the panel cannot be cleaned back once the cell is closed. Cleanroom lines and controlled handling are how that is managed during build.</p></details></div><div style="margin-top:40px;padding-top:26px;border-top:1px solid var(--line);"><a href="/markets/optical-sensors" class="hana-link" style="font-size:11px;letter-spacing:0.1em;"><i data-lucide="arrow-left" style="width:13px;height:13px;"></i> Back to the Optical &amp; Sensors market hub</a></div></div></section><section class="mk-cta" data-hana-bg="flow" data-variant="dark" data-fade="none"><div class="mk-wrap in"><div><h2 class="mk-h2 on-dark" style="max-width:520px;">Talk to us about your microdisplay program</h2><p class="mk-body on-dark" style="font-size:16px;max-width:480px;">Tell us what you need built and we will come back with a plan.</p></div><div class="acts"><a href="/contact" class="hana-btn hana-btn-on-dark" style="padding:12px 22px;font-size:12px;">Contact us <i data-lucide="arrow-right" style="width:14px;height:14px;margin-left:6px;"></i></a></div></div></section></div></div>
